  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap between a step function and its shifted versions. The previous approach used symmetric functions and simulated annealing, which may get stuck in local optima. We introduce an asymmetric constructive approach using genetic programming with a multi-population strategy to explore diverse solutions. The core idea is to combine several base tile patterns and optimize their arrangement through evolutionary operations, breaking symmetry that constrained the previous approach. Key limitations include the requirement to handle continuous grids (discretized in the code) and the need for efficient repair mechanisms.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h is a step function on [0,2], represented by n_points samples with sum(h)*dx=1, where dx=2/n_points and sum(h)=n_points/2.\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed via linear correlation with exact edge handling.\n- Repair: Enforce [0,1] bounds and integral constraint using a convex combination method that preserves feasibility.\n- Optimization: Population of parent functions combining base tiles (0-triangle, 1-rectangle, 2-trapezoid). Each function evolved via:\n  - Hyper-crossover mixing entire elevation/transition patterns\n  - Reflection mutation \n  - Adaptive repair with sliding window for boundary adjustments\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Initialize population with triangular shapes from parent/gauss convolution\n   b. For new_candidates:\n        - Hyper-crossover: Mix parent elevation functions while depth-similarity >0.4\n        - Reflection: Randomly flip direction\n        - Repair: Scale and clip to bounds\n        - Validate: Check continuity at steps using sliding min/max windows\n   c. Fitness: Negative of compute_overlap with exact window-normalization\n   d. Selection: Quality-proportional tournament selection with diversity bonus\n   e. Post-evaluation: Simple discretization to n_points resolution\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Hyper-xover rates: Pareto schedule =0.3 at 0.7 at 0.9 progression\n- Repair bias: Proportion of boundary-grown solutions up to 80%\n- Mutation: Reflection vector-parameterization with temperature mutation\n- Diversity: Maximum\u6238\u6e21 operations per candidate to prevent premature convergence\n- Repair variants: Apply sliding window independently at each repair stage\n\n5. ComputeBudget andRobustness\n- Dominant cost: Population-scale correlation (O(n\u00b2) for n=280 yielding ~600ms/candidate)\n- Time estimates: 20 candidates/sec for base operations (150 candidates fit within budget)\n- Fail-safes: Parent preservation of slope information (45% fallback success rates)\n- Variance handling: Multi-stage repair with fallback interval\n\n6. Validation andReturnContract\n- Sanitize input: Validate parent_gauss shape for continuity assumption\n- Objective check: x*y<=h(x) for all x\n- Feasibility tests:\n  - Array bounds: min(h)>=0, max(h)<=1\n  - Integral: sum(h)==n_points/2 within 1e-8\n- Additional checks:\n  - Correct padding: np.correlate with 'full'\n  - Computed C5 strictly via dx*(window-normalized correlation)\n\nImplementation structure:\n  - Use numpy for vectorized operations\n  - Time budget: Monitor CPU time using timeit\n  - Population size: 50 vectorizations at start, adaptive by fitness\n  - Mutation/crossover pairs: Process (query function) / (Pareto schedule)",
